d by offline processing during  the day  and it is you know ingested into a redis  instance and that essentially acts as  the primary store for  the applications using it so it's super  important for that layer to be highly  available  and for that particular use case it is  also important to ensure that  the instance is very highly available  but you also want to ensure that the  data is backed up  uh in case there is a counter-strike  failure so that you can recover  um using the backup um when the failure  happens  and lastly capacity cache is interesting  from the perspective of how customers  deploy the cache so one of the key  things that  capacity cache is used for is to  basically front a database  and to ensure that the back-end store is  not impacted as much so what that means  is that when you deploy your capacity  cache  you want to split your key space across  a large number of nodes  and so that even if one or two node  fails the  the impact on the back end um so  back end database is constrained and you  don't really  get into the thundering hub problem so  this is where the memcache deployment  can help because now you can spread your  data across  a large number of nodes and minimize the  blast radius  if a node goes down so these are some of  the four different  caching patterns that we see so what i  want to talk about now  is if your application use case  fits into one of these caching patterns  what are some of the best practices that  you should be following to ensure that  you're running the memory store instance  optimally  for your application workload so  the first thing that i would like you to  do is to understand  and follow the memory management best  practices for redis  one of the things that redis is  notorious for is for memory management  but as a managed service we do a lot of  things behind the scenes to ensure that  we take care of some of the  inefficiencies in terms of memory  management  that happens in redis for you but havi