he transient store and the primary  store and for  and i'll talk more about the  characteristics of these patterns in the  next slide  but along with the patterns there is  also the workload pattern which  essentially determines the scale in  terms of data set size  and the qps the read write ratio for the  application when it comes to  accessing the cache and also the uh the  payload size  so the combination of the workload  patterns along with the caching patterns  determines  what type of deployment you should be  doing for your particular use case  so let's take a look at the different  caching patterns and what that really  means  so ephemeral cache you can think of it  as a very simple cache if the cache goes  away  you can rehydrate the cache from a  backend store there is no  real impact to the application except  for maybe a slow performance small  performance degradation that is  not significant from an application  perspective  and for these kind of use cases it may  be enough to just deploy  you know memcache or the basic tier of  redis which is actually very very cheap  but when it comes to the transient store  use case it becomes a little bit more  interesting  so you have a use case like session  store where you have  you want to store the keys for a certain  amount of time  but you want the key keys to be  available for that period of time  because if you lose the key then the  user experience suffers  again it is not catastrophic but again  you don't but again the user experience  can get impacted which can also  translate  to translate to um bad customer  satisfaction and customers probably  you know leaving your service so you  want to make sure that when you have  use cases like session store your keys  are highly available  the primary store becomes an even more  interesting use case where  it is a primary store that is backing  your for example your front-end  um website and that data doesn't exist  anywhere else the data is typically  processed by offline processing during  the day  and it is you know ingested into a redis  instance and that essentially acts as  the primary store for  the applications using it so it's super  important for that layer to be highly  available  and for that particular use case it is  also important to ensure that  the instance is very highly available  but you also want to ensure that the  data is backed up  uh in case there is a counter-strike  failure so that you can recover  um using the backup um when the failure  happens  and lastly capacity cache is interesting  from the perspective of how customers  deploy the cache so one of the key  things that  capacity cache is used for is to  basically front a database  and to ensure that the back-end store is  not impacted as much so what that means  is that when you deploy your capacity  cache  you want to split your key space across  a large number of nodes  and so that even if one or two node  fails the  the impact on the back end um so  back end database is constrained and you  don't really  get into the thundering hub problem so  this is where the memcache deployment  can help because now you can spread your  data across  a large number of nodes and minimize the  blast radius  if a node goes down so these are some of  the four different  caching patterns that we see so what i  want to talk about now  is if your application use case  fits into one of these caching patterns  what are some of the best practices that  you should be following to ensure that  you're running the memory store instance  optimally  for your application workload so  the first thing that i would like you to  do is to understand  and follow the memory management best  practices for redis  one of the things that redis is  notorious for is for memory management  but as a managed service we do a lot of  things behind the scenes to ensure that  we take care of some of the  inefficiencies in terms of memory  management  that happens in redis for you but having  said that  it is super important for you to follow  the best practices  and and especially follow you know  monitoring the right metrics to ensure  that  your instance is running optimally  so let's look take a look at some of the  metrics that you should be monitoring  so we have there are four key metrics  and from our perspective that you should  be monitoring if you are using memory so  when it comes to memory usage the first  one is a system memory usage ratio  the system memory usage ratio basically  tells you  how much system memory is getting used  in your instance  so system memory also includes memory  that is consumed by memory fragmentation  so if your instance or your workload is  causing a lot  of memory fragmentation then the system  memory usage ratio will tell you  whether the fragmentation is higher or  not so  you absolutely should monitor system  memory usage ratio and ideally set a  threshold of about 80  so that you can start making right  sizing your instances  uh once that threshold is hit the other  one is memory usage ratio that basically  tells you  whether your deployed uh instance  is basically being used optimally from a  data usage perspective  the next one is also very interesting  and something that we introduced  recently  which is the time and system memory  overload so what happens behind the  scenes is that  we have put a cap in terms of system  memory beyond which we start blocking  rights  so when we do that the whole idea is  that we  ensure that there is enough system  memory available and the system will not  go into an unstable state or  crash but the experience that you will  get when you hit that limit  is essentially out of memory errors on  your applications when you try to do  rights  so one of the things that you want to  monitor is that is your instance getting  into that condition  um or not right it could be that there  are some  peak workloads where you briefly get  into that condition but then  the steady state is actually you know  okay  but it's something to keep monitoring  which will allow you to  determine whether you need to go right  size your instance or not  and lastly evictions is an interesting  good thing to monitor to ensure that if  you have  you know eviction policies set where you  expect the keys to be evicted you want  to monitor that  you know either the ttl is set or the  keys are getting evicted so that the  memory is managed optimally  so we highly recommend that if you're  using memory store  you always monitor these metrics and set  thresholds or rather alerts that  can proactively tell you whether your  instance is  getting into a bad state or not so  once you've done the memory usage uh  best practices and the monitoring around  that  the next thing you want to do is based  on the signals that you get  you want to be able to right size your  instance so like i mentioned earlier  the system memory usage ratio is a very  good indicator of how well  your system or instance is performing in  terms of memory consumption  so if you go beyond 80 what you would  want to do  is you want to bright size your instance  by lowering the max memory  um flag which we have provided as a  gcloud option  which will allow you to give more  overhead for the for the instance  this is super important especially when  you are doing things like backup or you  are scaling the instance  often because when you do a backup or  when you scale the instance we do kick  off  an rdb process which essentially  is a child process that we spin up  that can consume you know even double  the memory that you're using depending  on the right rate on your  on your uh instance so especially if  your  instance is a right heavy instance we  highly recommend that you provide  higher more overhead by lowering max  memory  to ensure that operations like backup  and scaling  can happen successfully  so that's about right sizing uh the  instance so  just to recap monitor your memory  management best practices  and right size based on the thresholds  that you see for system memory usage  ratio  or the metric around  time and out of memory condition  and finally i just wanted to touch upon  some general best practices that you  should be following  especially for the primary story use  case so one of the things that i  mentioned earlier is that  the memory store in instances today both  basic and sanitary instances are  single primary meaning  all your application queries are going  against a single  uh primary so what that means is that  you want to be able to run your  benchmark for your application and  understand whether  the um the qps especially the qps is  hitting the limits  um the other thing i would say is that  if you have a write  intensive workload uh meaning if it is  you know 80 20 reads versus 50 50 you  know  read write you really want to monitor  your memory usage  like um and follow the best practices  that i mentioned in the previous slide  it is super critical you do that so that  you can keep your instance right size  and not only that you can also determine  whether you're hitting the limits of the  memory for instance  one of the ways you can hit the limits  of the memory for instance and determine  whether you're  hitting the limits of the memories for  instance is by monitoring the  replication lab  so if there's a lot of rights and if  replication is not keeping up  then what can potentially happen is that  you're giving failover you can lose some  keys that has not been replicated  um and you can basically  run into scenarios where there's a lot  of key space loss if the replication lag  is too much so you basically want to  monitor  and lastly if you're using it as a  primary store you absolutely should take  a backup of your instance  althea published a paper um on how to  schedule  backups using cloud scheduler so that is  um that is published in our solution  website  and we have also published a solution  paper around  best practices around performance from  memory store redis  in general so i'd highly recommend if  you're using memory search today or  thinking about using memory store  that you follow the best practices  around memory management  and you follow you monitor the metrics  that is recommended  and also learn how to take actions  depending on what the metrics are  telling you  so by doing that we are essentially  being proactive  about ensuring that the memory store  instance run in a stable state  and it also more importantly allows you  to determine  whether the memory store uh instance  is actually a suitable for your  workloads  in some cases if you find the memory  store instances are not  able to keep up then you will have to  shard your memory store  or application workload across multiple  memory store instances  using client-side shopping so that  hopefully gives you a general  understanding of what are some of the  best practices that you should be  following when using memory store  and i hope that you find it useful and  and  it is something that you can put into  practice immediately  so with that i just want to touch upon  uh  some useful links that will help you  um understand a lot more about what i've  just  talked about here so if you there's a  memory management  best practices documentation uh that we  have published  we have published the performance during  best practices and we also have  published a solution paper  on how to schedule backups  using cloud scheduler um all this  together hopefully it will give you  a great start in terms of running your  memory store instance optimally  thank you very much  you
 

